From uab8277113f65c05e at groups.google.com  Thu Nov  7 10:00:18 2013
From: uab8277113f65c05e at groups.google.com (Altuna Akalin)
Date: Thu, 07 Nov 2013 10:00:18 +0000
Subject: Re: [methylkit_discussion] advice on using methylkit
Message-ID: <GyxOvoBzQrc.RHKSMthqbUoJ@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <GyxOvoBzQrc.qwh2fT3nw7gJ@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
References: <GyxOvoBzQrc.qwh2fT3nw7gJ@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/methylkit_discussion/c/GyxOvoBzQrc/m/RHKSMthqbUoJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

do:
myobj[[2][ is.na <http://is.na>(myobj[[2]]$numCs/myobj[[2]]$coverage),]

and check those rows that generate NA/NaN values. The problem might be that maybe NA character is not recognised by R, maybe they are using a different one. Or maybe, there is a division by 0 creates NaN values. In any case, the code above gets you the rows that are problematic. And you can figure out what to do from there on. If NaN values are created, somehow coverage 0 bases are in your file. Check if you are using the correct column from BSMAP output. If there are other NA characters that you are not filtering for, filter them as well.

Best,
Altuna

